  "summary": "I wrote the endless runner in `src/main.ts` and restructured `index.html`. It typechecks, but I couldn't run `vite build` or open it in a browser, so it hasn't been run or seen rendered.\n\n- **Scene:** The runner is a character built from shapes. It has swinging legs and arms, a forward lean and a tucked pose in the air. It stays at the origin and the world scrolls past a fixed chase camera. Speed cues are track stripes, centre dashes, side posts and trees, with fog hiding the spawn point.\n- **Hurdles and rules:** The game draws random hurdles of varying height and spacing from a seeded generator. The seed is 1337, or `?seed=N` in the URL. Restarts reuse the same seed, so a run repeats exactly.\n- **Jump and speed:** Jumping follows a gravity arc and is blocked while airborne. Space, ArrowUp, W and a pointer click or tap all jump. Speed rises from 8 m/s at 0 m to 18 m/s at 500 m.\n- **Flow:** The game starts in `ready` and begins on the first jump. Touching a hurdle sets `lost`. Reaching 500 m sets `won`.\n- **Restart:** R returns to `ready` at 0 m. A jump after the run ends restarts and starts playing straight away. The task text didn't say which of those a jump should do, so I picked this.\n- **HUD and state:** The HUD sits below the canvas and shows distance, session best and status. A game-over overlay with a restart hint covers the canvas. To hold the overlay I wrapped the canvas in a fixed 640x360 `#stage` div in `index.html`. The canvas size is unchanged.\n- **Globals:** `window.__TASK_STATE__` is updated every frame and on each input. `window.__TASK_READY__` is set after the first frames have rendered, with a fallback after about 120 animation frames if the engine callback never fires.\n\n",
